\section*{Zero–One Law for Directional Transience}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: The directional zero--one conclusion is claimed for uniformly elliptic stationary finite-range-dependent environments in dimensions at least three, including irrational fixed directions; Iid strictly elliptic walks on $\mathbb Z^d$, $d\geq3$, are claimed to have escape probability either zero or one in each predetermined nonzero direction; Iid uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor walks in dimensions $d\geq2$ are claimed to have positive directional speed whenever escape in that direction is almost sure. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Let \(d\ge3\) and consider nearest-neighbor transition rows on \(\mathbb Z^d\) under a translation-stationary ergodic environment law \(Q\). Assume a deterministic ellipticity lower bound \(\omega(x,e)\ge\kappa>0\) almost surely and a deterministic dependence range \(R\): row sigma-fields for any two deterministic sets, including infinite sets, are independent when their \(\ell^\infty\) distance exceeds \(R\). For the annealed law \(P_0=\int P_{0,\omega}\,dQ\), the claimed conclusion is
\[
 \forall\ell\in\mathbb R^d\setminus\{0\}\text{ fixed},\qquad
 P_0(X_n\cdot\ell\to+\infty)\in\{0,1\}.
\]

\paragraph{Result 2.} Let \(d\ge3\). Assume the nearest-neighbor transition rows on \(\mathbb Z^d\) are independent with the same law, and every entry in a row is strictly positive almost surely. There need not be a common positive lower bound. For each fixed nonzero direction and the annealed walk law, the source claims
\[
 P_0(X_n\cdot\ell\to+\infty)\in\{0,1\}.
\]
Strict positivity, iid rows, and the dimension condition are retained.

\paragraph{Result 3.} On \(\mathbb Z^d\), take iid nearest-neighbor transition rows with a deterministic lower bound \(\kappa>0\) on every transition probability. Let \(P_0\) be the environment-averaged walk law and \(A_\ell=\{X_n\cdot\ell\to+\infty\}\). For \(d\ge2\), the main claim is
\[
 P_0(A_\ell)=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \exists v\in\mathbb R^d\text{ deterministic}:\
 v\cdot\ell>0,\quad P_0(X_n/n\to v)=1.
\]
For \(d\ge3\), the corollary replaces the premise by \(P_0(A_\ell)>0\). If any such direction exists, then for each fixed \(u\in S^{d-1}\),
\[
 P_0(A_u)=1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ P_0(A_u)>0\ \Longleftrightarrow\ v\cdot u>0.
\]
It does not assert a simultaneous pathwise statement for uncountably many directions.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The proof uses independent sigma-fields of separated transition rows rather than an iid-label representation. Buffered regeneration pieces keep reveals beyond the dependence range, allowing the directional contact and escape contradiction to survive the finite-range correlations.

Fresh-row and recurrent-site trials provide slab escape without a uniform transition lower bound. Regeneration-width estimates first produce the possible escape rays and show they are opposite; contact comparisons between regenerated path arrangements exclude coexistence of the two signs.

Regeneration is split into spatial-radius and temporal estimates. Controlling radii first yields quantitative crossing information without assuming nearby directional transience, and the resulting tail estimates make regeneration duration integrable, producing a deterministic positive speed.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The directional zero–one law holds for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments on Zd, d>=3, with independence for separated infinite row sets. The problem statement omits all these hypotheses, and its background also names a two-dimensional invariance-principle question; The theorem proves the one-direction annealed zero–one law for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor rows on Zd for d>=3, requiring no uniform lower bound or moments. The literal problem statement imposes neither iid nor ellipticity nor a dimension restriction, and its background includes a separate two-dimensional invariance-principle question. The cited corollary proves a directional zero–one law and the exact velocity hemisphere for iid uniformly elliptic environments in d>=3. It does not cover arbitrary dependent random environments or the additional two-dimensional invariance-principle issue in the problem statement background. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 220, the documented iid results include strict-ellipticity directional zero--one laws, uniform-ellipticity ballisticity, and the positive-velocity hemisphere. The later finite-range-dependent environment theorem is not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{DirectionalBallisticity.lean}, \texttt{DirectionalWalk.lean}, \texttt{VelocityHemisphere.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirectionalTransience.directional\_transience\_implies\_ballisticity}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

